# The preset policy in kgsm-base decides whether the assistant runs on a node; applying it is
# symlink manipulation on disk, so it works during the transaction and in a chroot alike. Starting
# it is the node's post-transaction hook's job, and this unit needs nothing from a person first.
#
# post_install only. An administrator who disabled this unit keeps it disabled across every later
# version — re-presetting on upgrade would quietly undo that.

post_install() {
    systemctl preset kgsm-assistant-service.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

    cat <<'MSG'

  kgsm-assistant is enabled and starts on its own. It generates the key its sign-ins are signed
  with on that first start, in /var/lib/kgsm-assistant/signing-key, and reuses it forever after.
  Set Auth__SigningKey in /etc/kgsm-assistant/service.env only to pin one deliberately.

  It needs a model server it can reach — Ollama, or the llama.cpp units in kgsm-llm-llamacpp.
  Every turn fails without one. Keep Ollama bound to 127.0.0.1: a routable bind is an
  unauthenticated inference endpoint on the internet.

  Install kgsm-rag-indexer to build the retrieval index. Without it the assistant still answers — it
  just has no local corpus to draw on.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  If pacman wrote a .pacnew beside /etc/kgsm-assistant/service.env, merge it — new settings are
  declared there and a key the file never declares binds to nothing.

MSG
}
